      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_057CEE13_CONCEPTS_01_FRAME_AND_POSITION",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR_FRAME_AND_POSITION",
          "title": "1. Frame of Reference and Coordinate Position",
          "contentMarkdown": "To specify the position of an object, a **reference point** (origin) and a set of coordinate axes are required. A coordinate system along with a clock constitutes a physical frame of reference. The coordinates $(x, y, z)$ describe the position of the object with respect to this coordinate system. In one-dimensional rectilinear motion, only one coordinate axis (e.g. $x$-axis) is needed.",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_057CEE13_2052DE05",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_057CEE13_9CA8922D",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_057CEE13_7E573255",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_057CEE13_CD4D2C0C"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_057CEE13_CONCEPTS_02_PATH_LENGTH_VS_DISPLACEMENT",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR_PATH_LENGTH_VS_DISPLACEMENT",
          "title": "2. Path Length (Distance) vs. Displacement",
          "contentMarkdown": "- **Path Length**: the total distance traversed by an object during its motion in a given time interval. It is a scalar quantity (magnitude only, always non-negative).\n- **Displacement ($\\Delta x$)**: the change in position vector of an object in a given time interval: $\\Delta x = x_2 - x_1$. It is a vector quantity having both magnitude and direction.\nThe magnitude of displacement is always less than or equal to the total path length traversed ($|\\Delta x| \\le \\text{Path Length}$).",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_057CEE13_E54E59B1",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_057CEE13_A000AC8C",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_057CEE13_2771DF7B",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_057CEE13_DE97A799",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_057CEE13_F8D15ED0",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_057CEE13_3FBCF3EC"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_057CEE13_CONCEPTS_03_AVERAGE_SPEED_AND_VELOCITY",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR_AVERAGE_SPEED_AND_VELOCITY",
          "title": "3. Average Speed and Average Velocity",
          "contentMarkdown": "- **Average Speed ($s_{\\text{avg}}$)**: total path length divided by the total time interval: $s_{\\text{avg}} = \\frac{\\text{Total Path Length}}{\\Delta t}$.\n- **Average Velocity ($v_{\\text{avg}}$)**: net displacement divided by the time interval: $v_{\\text{avg}} = \\frac{\\Delta x}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{x_2 - x_1}{t_2 - t_1}$.\nAverage velocity can be positive, negative, or zero, while average speed is always positive for a moving body.",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_057CEE13_41439691",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_PHY_057CEE13_BAB1AAFF"
          ]
        }
      ],
